{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Recorded a durable correction rather than a new finding. The behaviour entry describing ReadOnlySafe admission said each admitted request broadcasts a heartbeat, which reads as one message per request; the isolated-network receipt shows the cost is one heartbeat per other tracked peer, that is n-1 messages per request - 64 for 32 requests in a three-voter cluster. Since that entry is used by the asserted read-index defect account and by several interpretations that reasoned about retry cost, I corrected its premise under the same id rather than adding a parallel entry: the revised statement spells out that sendMsgReadIndexResponse admits the request and then calls bcastHeartbeat, so a request costs one heartbeat per peer, and it cites the measured 32 admitted requests with confirmed zero and 64 heartbeats as the execution evidence. The changed_premise field explains the semantic difference from the earlier revision, and affected_uses lists the recorded experiment and interpretation records that relied on the old reading, verified against the controller's own list of valid use ids. The two asserted defects and their receipts, the measured StateSnapshot repair exits, and the explained or refuted threads are otherwise unchanged; the correction tightens the read-index amplification claim from one message per retry to one per peer per retry.\"}"}}
